# cat Command (Concatenate and Display)

The `cat` command is used to **view the contents of a file**, **concatenate multiple files**, or **create new files**.

---

## Basic Usage

```bash
cat file.txt

  
## cat Command Summary

| Command | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `cat file.txt` | display the contents of a file |
| `cat file1.txt file2.txt` | display multiple files one after another |
| `cat file1.txt > newfile.txt` | write/overwrite to a new file |
| `cat file1.txt >> existingfile.txt` | append contents to an existing file |
| `cat -n file.txt` | display file with line numbers |


  ## cat Command Examples

| Command | Description | Example Output |
|--------|-------------|----------------|
| `cat file.txt` | display the contents of a file | shows all lines in `file.txt` |
| `cat file1.txt file2.txt` | display multiple files | contents of `file1.txt` followed by `file2.txt` |
| `cat file1.txt > combined.txt` | write/overwrite to a new file | creates `combined.txt` containing both files |
| `cat file1.txt >> existing.txt` | append contents to an existing file | adds `file1.txt` to the end of `existing.txt` |
| `cat -n file.txt` | display file with line numbers | each line is numbered from 1 upwards |

